# 351-APP-0088 — `files` reads the kernel from the slot that booted — done

**Type:** tools (`files`) · **Owner:** tools track (`APP`) · **Priority:** P3 · **Status:** done (2026-10-20) · **Blocked by:** — · **Main task:** [351](../issues/351-self-update.md) · **Roadmap:** track E · **Constitution:** MC-12.1

**Recorded by:** the update track (`UPD`), 2026-10-11, in `requests-APP.md`, for [351-UPD-0016](351-UPD-0016-images-with-slots.done). Numbered by the tools track on 2026-10-20, at the maintainer's word.

## Problem

`files` reads `kernel.elf` and the bootloader to show that whole files come back through `vfs_server`. On an image made with `make_usb_image.py --slots` the kernel is in `MIND/A/kernel.elf` (or `MIND/B/` after an update), not at the root, so `files` reports `OPEN kernel.elf FAILED`.

## Plan

- Read `kernel.elf` from the root when it is there, else from the slot that booted (`BootInfo.boot_slot`), `MIND/A` or `MIND/B`.
- Say which one was read.

## Acceptance criteria

- On the slot image of `tests/usb_image_smoke.py`, `files` reads the slot's kernel whole and names its path.
- On the plain image, it reads the root's as before.

## Resolution

- **`files/src/main.rs`:** reads `kernel.elf` from the root when it is there; otherwise, on a boot from slot A or B (`BootInfo.boot_slot`), `MIND/A/kernel.elf` or `MIND/B/kernel.elf`, and its line ends `(THE SLOT THAT BOOTED)`.
- **`tests/usb_image_smoke.py`, the slot image:** `files` reads `MIND/A/kernel.elf` whole: `READ MIND/A/kernel.elf <size>/<size> BYTES MAGIC=7F454C46`, its size from the packed payload. The check joins the line the shell's console wrapped and reads the slot from the path (f5fe0395, 3ab0e293). The plain image's `files_check` stays as it was.
- **In `main`** since 835c31c8 (2026-10-20). The x86 USB image test passed on i9 on 3ab0e293; the rest of the gate as for the batch (every x86 group on i9, every aarch64 group on un-rizen-service, the host tests and five x86 groups locally).
- This is QEMU's USB stick on xHCI with OVMF, not a real machine's (MC-12.1).

